{
  "dom_id": "step-q87918",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $(5-x)^{2}+4\\le 6.$",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-x)^{2}+4 & \\le 6 \\\\ (5-x)^{2} & \\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(5-x)^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ |5-x| & \\le \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2}\\le 5-x\\le \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\le 5-x & \\le \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}-5\\le -x & \\le \\sqrt{2}-5 \\\\ 5+\\sqrt{2}\\ge x & \\ge 5-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the lower number is on the left:\n\n$$\n5-\\sqrt{2}\\le x\\le 5+\\sqrt{2}\n$$",
